import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const regCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '30 + 0.8*x', label: 'fitted line: hours = 30 + 0.8·score', color: '#374151', width: 2.2 },
  ],
  points: [{
    points: [[40, 58], [45, 70], [50, 66], [55, 78], [60, 82], [65, 79], [70, 92], [75, 88], [80, 97], [85, 101]],
    label: 'students', mode: 'dots', color: '#b45309',
  }],
  x: { min: 35, max: 90, label: 'exam score' },
  y: { min: 40, max: 110, label: 'study hours (month)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const regQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'A line of best fit for revision time (hours, x) against test score (y) is y = 37 + 5.9x. What does the 5.9 mean?',
      choices: [
        'The maximum possible score',
        'Each extra hour of revision is associated with about 5.9 more marks, on average, within the measured range — the gradient is the model\'s exchange rate between the variables',
        'Every student gains exactly 5.9 marks',
        'The test has 5.9 questions',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The gradient is a per-unit prediction, not a guarantee: individual students scatter around the line, and the word "associated" is doing essential work — the regression says how y moves with x in this data, not what caused what. The intercept (37) predicts the score at zero hours, meaningful only if x = 0 is inside or near the data.',
    },
    {
      q: 'The fitted line above is used to predict the score for 60 hours of revision, when the data ran from 1 to 8 hours. This is:',
      choices: [
        'Fine — lines work anywhere',
        'Extrapolation, and the classic regression error: the linear pattern is verified only inside the observed range, and outside it the true relationship may curve, plateau or reverse — 60 hours predicts about 391 marks, which is impossible',
        'More accurate than interpolation',
        'Only wrong if r is small',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The line has no knowledge beyond the data that built it. Predicting 431 at 60 hours is arithmetic, not evidence. The syllabus limitation is exactly this: use the line to interpolate within the measured range; treat anything outside as a hypothesis needing new data.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A dataset of (height, shoe size) has r = 0.97; a dataset of (daily temperature, ice-cream sales) has r = 0.85; a dataset of (shoe size, maths score) has r ≈ 0. What does each r measure?',
      choices: [
        'How fast one variable causes the other',
        'The strength and direction of straight-line association only — strong for the first two (whatever the cause), essentially none for the third; r says nothing about causation and nothing about curved relationships',
        'The slope of the line in each case',
        'The reliability of the measurements',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'r is dimensionless, from −1 to +1, and measures linear co-movement. Two datasets can share r = 0.85 with completely different stories: a third variable (season) drives the ice-cream one; genetics and age drive the first. And r ≈ 0 does not prove unrelatedness — a perfect U-shaped curve also scores near zero.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does the "method of semi-averages" (split the data by x into halves, average each half, draw the line through the two mean points) give a sensible line?',
      choices: [
        'It is easier to teach than any other method and has no logic',
        'Each mean point is the balance point of its half, so the line through both honours the average behaviour early and late — a robust, teachable approximation to the least-squares line',
        'It always passes through every point',
        'It ignores outliers entirely',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Averages swallow local noise, so the two mean-points summarise each half faithfully; the line joining them captures the overall trend with almost no computation. Least squares refines it (using every point, minimising squared vertical errors), but for estimating trends by hand — and for building the intuition that regression lines pass through the data\'s centre of gravity — semi-averages is the honest quick method.',
    },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/intermediate/correlation-and-regression">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson adds a second variable to everything <strong>Describing Data</strong> taught
    you about single data sets: from here on, statistics asks how two measurements move
    together. The scatter diagram, the correlation coefficient and the fitted line all reuse
    the centres, the spread measures and the plot-reading judgement you already practised,
    while <strong>Probability Foundations</strong> returns as the reason a line drawn from a
    sample can still be trusted — and as the warning label on how far that trust extends.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>read and draw a scatter diagram, assigning the independent and dependent variables to match the question;</li>
    <li>judge strength and direction on the r scale, and know when r is lying — curves, outliers, Anscombe's quartet;</li>
    <li>draw a line of best fit by eye and by semi-averages, and explain what least squares improves;</li>
    <li>use a fitted line to predict, and say precisely where interpolation ends and extrapolation begins;</li>
    <li>refuse the two classic fallacies: correlation is causation, and lines extend forever.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The page works one dataset — revision hours against exam scores — by hand from scatter
    to prediction, so every abstraction lands on numbers you computed yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Correlation</strong> measures whether two variables move together (−1 to +1);
    <strong>regression</strong> draws the best straight line through the data so you can predict
    one from the other. Together they are statistics's most used — and most misused —
    pair of tools: the same mathematics runs in epidemiology, econometrics, A/B tests
    and machine learning, and the same two fallacies (correlation is causation; lines
    extend forever) ambush the unwary in every field. The line is chosen by <strong>least
    squares</strong> — minimising the sum of squared vertical gaps, a method Gauss and
    Legendre published in the early 1800s and every spreadsheet still runs — but the
    route to it runs through skills this syllabus names: reading a scatter diagram,
    judging correlation by eye, drawing a line by eye and by semi-averages, and
    knowing exactly what the line's equation may and may not be asked. This page
    covers all of it, with one dataset worked by hand from scatter to prediction.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="does more study predict higher scores? the line says yes — the scatter says how confidently" widget="plotter" config={regCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the scatter shows ten students; the grey line asserts
    the trend (hours = 30 + 0.8·score). Watch how the points sit around the line — hugging
    it through the middle of the range, scattering wider at the ends — because that
    dispersion is the honest error bar on any single prediction. Then notice what the plot
    cannot show: the direction of causation. Strong students may simply study more; the
    diagram records the association and stays silent about which variable drives which.
  </p>

  <p>
    The correlation here is strong (r ≈ 0.95): points hug the line. For every extra exam point,
    the line predicts 0.8 more study hours. Two cautions the data itself can't resolve: this is
    an <em>association</em> (maybe strong students just study more), and the pattern holds only
    within the measured range.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> correlation and regression are the paired tools for bivariate
    data — r compresses the co-movement into one number, the line exploits it for
    prediction. Next: the scatter diagram itself, where the axes must be assigned before
    any line is legal.
  </p>

  <h2>Scatter diagrams and the two variables</h2>
  <p>
    Bivariate data — pairs of measurements on the same subjects — lives on a
    <strong>scatter diagram</strong>, one dot per subject. Before any line is drawn,
    two decisions give the axes their meaning. The <strong>independent variable</strong>
    (explanatory, x-axis) is the one you treat as the input — hours of revision,
    temperature, advertising spend; the <strong>dependent variable</strong>
    (response, y-axis) is the one being predicted — score, ice-cream sales.
    The convention follows the logic: we predict the dependent <em>from</em> the
    independent, and regression lines are read vertically (given x, what y?).
    Choosing which is which is not always mechanical — either variable can
    be predicted from the other, with different resulting lines — but the
    question you intend to ask should pick the axes. The scatter itself then
    speaks in shapes: upward drift (positive association), downward
    (negative), curved (the linear tools are the wrong tool), banded and
    diffuse (weak), or point-clouds with no pattern (none). The professional
    habit the syllabus is quietly teaching: <strong>plot first, compute second</strong> —
    every horror story in this page begins with someone skipping the plot.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> which variable goes on which axis is a
  free choice with no consequences. The convention follows the question:
  the explanatory variable sits on the x-axis and the response on the
  y-axis so the line reads as "given x, predict y" — swap them and you
  are answering a different question, and least squares will draw you a
  genuinely different line, not the same one tipped on its side.</p>

  <h2>Correlation: strength and direction on the r scale</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>correlation coefficient r</strong> compresses a scatter into one number
    between −1 and +1: <em>direction</em> from the sign, <em>straight-line
    strength</em> from the magnitude. Rough calibration: |r| above about
    0.8 is strong, 0.5–0.8 moderate, 0.2–0.5 weak, below 0.2 negligible —
    with the caveat that "strong" is contextual (r = 0.5 between a
    psychological measure and a behaviour can be a major finding; r = 0.5
    between two physics lab instruments is a calibration failure). r = +1
    means perfect positive linear alignment, r = −1 perfect negative, and
    r ≈ 0 means <em>no linear association</em> — a phrase whose precision
    matters, since a perfect U-shaped curve also scores r ≈ 0 (the
    coefficient only measures straight-line co-movement). Three reading
    rules: r is dimensionless (it ignores units — heights in metres or
    inches give the same r); r measures association, not agreement
    (x and x + 10 correlate perfectly but differ by 10 everywhere); and r
    is unusually sensitive to outliers, because the deviations it feeds
    on get squared — one billionaire in an income study can move r by
    tenths. The classic demonstration is <strong>Anscombe's quartet</strong>
    (1973): four datasets — a clean linear trend, a perfect curve, an
    outlier-distorted cloud and a vertical line of x values — engineered
    to share the same r (0.816) and the same fitted line while being
    completely different data. Plot, always plot.
  </p>

  <h2>Lines by eye and by semi-averages</h2>
  <p>
    Before least squares, the syllabus's hand methods build the intuition
    that a regression line is a <em>summary</em> of the cloud. <strong>By eye:</strong>
    lay a transparent ruler on the scatter so the points are balanced
    above and below the line along its whole length — the line should
    pass through the general drift, with residuals (vertical gaps)
    cancelling at both ends, not fit a lucky subset. <strong>By the method of
    semi-averages:</strong> order the data by x, split into a lower half and an
    upper half, compute the mean x and mean y of each half, and draw the
    line through the two mean points. <strong>Worked example.</strong> Eight
    students' revision hours (x) and scores (y): (1, 45), (2, 51), (3, 54),
    (4, 58), (5, 66), (6, 69), (7, 73), (8, 80). Lower half means:
    x̄₁ = (1+2+3+4)/4 = 2.5, ȳ₁ = (45+51+54+58)/4 = 52. Upper half:
    x̄₂ = 6.5, ȳ₂ = (66+69+73+80)/4 = 72. Line through (2.5, 52) and
    (6.5, 72): gradient = (72 − 52) ÷ (6.5 − 2.5) = 20 ÷ 4 = <strong>5 marks per
    hour</strong>; intercept: 52 = a + 5 × 2.5 gives a = 39.5. So
    y ≈ 39.5 + 5x — a line that honours the average behaviour of both
    halves. Why it works: each half's mean is that half's balance point,
    so the line joining them summarises the trend with the noise averaged
    out. Why least squares improves on it: the semi-average line uses
    eight data points only through two summaries, while least squares
    uses every point, choosing the gradient and intercept that minimise
    the sum of <em>squared</em> vertical errors (squaring both prevents
    positive and negative gaps cancelling and penalises big misses) —
    the method of Legendre (1805) and Gauss, and the reason the line
    always passes through the point (x̄, ȳ), the cloud's centre of gravity.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a good line of best fit must thread
  through as many of the plotted points as possible, or even pass
  through the origin. Neither is true: the line is a summary of the
  whole cloud, judged by how the residuals balance along its entire
  length, and it usually passes through few or none of the actual data
  points. The origin is just another point in the plane — the line
  crosses it only if zero on both axes lies inside the data and the
  fit happens to take it there.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> you can draw a summary line by eye or through two semi-average
    points, and least squares refines both by consulting every data point at once. Now the
    line goes to work as a predictor — under a checklist that says exactly where its
    authority ends.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the line: interpolation, prediction, and the limits</h2>
  <p>
    The fitted line's equation, y = a + bx, earns its keep as a predictor —
    with an interpretation for each part. <strong>The gradient b</strong> is the
    exchange rate: our worked line predicts about 5 extra marks per extra
    revision hour, <em>on average, within the measured range</em>. <strong>The
    intercept a</strong> predicts y at x = 0 — meaningful here (39.5 marks for
    zero revision), meaningless when x = 0 lies outside the data (a
    height–weight line's "weight at 0 cm height"). The syllabus's
    assumptions and limitations are the checklist for honest use:
    <strong>linearity</strong> (the pattern really is straight in the measured
    range — check the scatter for curves); <strong>independence of
    observations</strong> (each student counted once); <strong>the range
    restriction</strong> — <em>interpolation</em> (predicting inside the observed
    x-range) is supported by the data, while <strong>extrapolation</strong>
    (predicting outside it) assumes the pattern continues unverified:
    our line predicts 60 hours → 339.5 marks, past the exam's maximum —
    arithmetic, not evidence. The same error at scale: extrapolating a
    two-year trend line into next decade's climate, or a company's
    growth curve into a valuation. And one more caveat the residuals
    can reveal: prediction accuracy is about the <em>average</em> — the
    scatter around the line (measured by the residuals, or summarised
    as r², the fraction of y's variation the line explains) tells you
    how wide the honest error bar on any single prediction is.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a strong correlation establishes cause and
  effect. Correlation is evidence of association — no more. The
  ice-cream–drowning correlation is real and causal in neither direction
  (summer drives both); the shoe-size–reading-age correlation in children
  has a lurking variable (age); and reverse causation (does confidence
  cause success, or success confidence?) is invisible to r. Licensed
  "because" claims need experiments, controlled variables or the
  causal-inference machinery of the expert tier — the regression line
  reports how variables move together, and the <em>story</em> of why is
  foreign territory to it.</p>

  <h2>r² and the language of explained variation</h2>
  <p>
    The correlation coefficient has an offspring worth naming:
    <strong>r²</strong> (the coefficient of determination) measures the
    <em>fraction of the dependent variable's variation that the fitted
    line explains</em>. If revision hours and scores correlate at
    r = 0.9, then r² = 0.81 — 81% of the differences between students'
    scores travel with their differences in revision, leaving 19% to
    everything else (prior ability, sleep, exam-day luck). The r²
    scale is honest about what strong correlations do and do not
    deliver: r = 0.5 — which intuition calls "moderate" — is only
    r² = 0.25, a quarter of the story. It also disciplines prediction
    language: a regression with r² = 0.36 (r = 0.6) explains about a
    third of the variance, so its individual predictions carry error
    bars wider than the pattern might suggest — the widget above,
    with r ≈ 0.95, has r² ≈ 0.9, which is what "points hug the line"
    means numerically. When you next read "X explains 30% of the
    variation in Y", you are reading an r² wearing its practical
    clothes.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> r² converts the correlation into the fraction of the story the
    line actually tells — and disciplines the language of every "explains X% of the
    variation" claim you will read. One assumption underneath all of it remains untested:
    that the relationship is straight.
  </p>

  <h2>When the line is the wrong tool: curved relationships</h2>
  <p>
    Every tool on this page assumes the relationship is straight, and the
    most instructive failures are the curved ones. A classic worked
    shape: <strong>exam score against study hours</strong> rises steeply at
    first (the first hours buy the most marks), then flattens as
    fatigue and diminishing returns bite — an S or plateau curve.
    Fitted with a straight line, that data yields a mediocre r, a
    line that underpredicts the low-hours students and the
    high-hours students alike, and — worst — an <em>extrapolation
    absurdity in both directions</em> (zero hours never scores the
    intercept, and forty hours does not score 200). The diagnostic
    is always the <strong>residual plot</strong>: fit the line, then plot
    what it missed; a curved band of residuals (negative at both
    ends, positive in the middle, or vice versa) is the data
    filing a complaint. The remedies escalate: transform a
    variable (logarithms linearise many growth curves — the
    log scale of every pandemic chart), fit a quadratic term,
    or abandon lines for the appropriate curve. The syllabus's
    assumption check is exactly this habit: <em>linearity is not
    a given; it is a claim the scatter must support</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A fitted line for revision hours against score is y = 39.5 + 5x. What exactly does the 5 claim, and what does the 39.5 claim?</li>
    <li>Two datasets share r = 0.85. What can you conclude about how similar their stories are?</li>
    <li>Your line predicts 339.5 marks for 60 revision hours, but the data ran from 1 to 8 hours. What is this error called, and where else does it bite?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The gradient is the exchange rate — about 5 extra marks
    per extra hour, <em>on average, within the measured range</em>; the intercept predicts
    the score at zero hours, meaningful only because x = 0 sits inside this dataset.
    Neither number promises anything about an individual student, who scatters around the
    line. (2) Almost nothing: r measures straight-line strength and direction only — it
    says nothing about causation, nothing about curved relationships, and nothing about
    the lurking third variable that may drive both (season behind ice-cream and drowning).
    (3) Extrapolation — the linear pattern was verified only inside the observed range, so
    outside it the true relationship may curve, plateau or reverse; the same error inflates
    decade-long trend-line forecasts and company valuations alike.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Plot first, compute second — every misuse of regression begins with a skipped scatter diagram.</li>
    <li>r measures straight-line association from −1 to +1: direction from the sign, strength from the magnitude, nothing else.</li>
    <li>Least squares minimises squared vertical errors; the line always passes through (x̄, ȳ), the cloud's centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>The gradient is a per-unit exchange rate; the intercept speaks only when x = 0 lies inside or near the data.</li>
    <li>Interpolate inside the measured range; extrapolation is arithmetic, not evidence.</li>
    <li>r² is the fraction of the variation the line explains — r = 0.5 is only a quarter of the story.</li>
    <li>Correlation is evidence of association, never of causation; curved relationships file their complaint in the residual plot.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The question this page deliberately left open — is an association strong enough to be
    real, or luck of the sample? — is <strong>Hypothesis Testing</strong>'s subject, which
    supplies the significance test for r itself. <strong>Confidence Intervals</strong> puts
    error bars on the predictions this line makes, and <strong>Sampling &amp; Survey
    Design</strong> explains why a scatter is only ever as trustworthy as the sample it
    came from.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Outliers and leverage.</strong> Least squares squares errors, so a far-out
    x value (high <em>leverage</em>) can pivot the whole line while looking like
    one odd point. The professional checks: plot with and without the
    outlier, report both if the conclusion changes, and never delete an
    outlier silently — the deletion is sometimes the finding (the
    ozone-hole data's outlier-discarding software delayed its discovery).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Regression to the mean.</strong> Why do outstanding first years tend to
    disappoint in the second, and terrible ones improve? Partly because
    extreme performances mix skill with luck, and luck does not repeat —
    the regression effect Francis Galton named the field for, measuring it
    in parents' and children's heights. Any "improvement after intervention"
    claim on extreme groups must subtract it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Simpson's paradox.</strong> Aggregated data can show an association
    opposite to every subgroup's — Berkeley's 1973 admissions data showed
    men admitted at higher rates overall while most departments admitted
    women at higher rates (women applied to the most competitive
    departments). The scatter lesson scaled up: always ask what is being
    aggregated, because the line through the pooled cloud can be nobody's
    line at all.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Legendre (1805), Gauss (1809); Anscombe, The American Statistician (1973); OpenStax, Introductory Statistics; Galton's regression studies.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself" widget="quiz" config={regQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the questions trace the page's spine in order — what
    the gradient promises (an average tendency, not a guarantee for any individual), where
    the line's licence expires (60 hours past a 1-to-8-hour dataset), what r does and does
    not measure, and why the semi-average line is an honest hand-drawn summary. If the
    extrapolation item predicted 391 marks without triggering alarm, reread "Using the
    line": the range restriction is the checklist's most-violated clause.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
